# Packaging exclusions are DEFENSE IN DEPTH ONLY. The authoritative
# mechanism is the explicit allowlist in package.json "files": only the
# Linux build script, Linux source, built Linux helper, and its trust
# manifest are listed under scripts/native/, so no Windows candidate
# artifact can enter the tarball even if present in the working tree.
# .npmignore must never be relied on to exclude entries inside a broadly
# "files"-listed directory (npm cannot exclude such entries). When
# .npmignore exists, npm does NOT consult .gitignore, so this file must
# still carry every exclusion that matters for the published tarball.
#
# The built Linux native helper binary + trust manifest are gitignored build
# artifacts, but they MUST ship in the tarball: without them an installed
# consumer cannot use anchored filesystem operations and fails closed.
# They ship because package.json "files" lists them explicitly. The
# negations below state that inclusion explicitly, so a future change to
# the "files" list cannot silently drop them.
!scripts/native/vcp-linux-fsops
!scripts/native/vcp-linux-fsops.manifest.json

# NEVER ship Windows candidate artifacts. The Windows native helper is a
# separate, D-01-gated, unshipped candidate; a Linux tarball must not carry
# a Windows executable, and a Linux binary must never satisfy Windows.
scripts/native/vcp-windows-fsops.exe
scripts/native/vcp-windows-fsops.obj
build/

# Local/editor artefacts (mirrors .gitignore for packed directories).
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.tmp
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
